
<handout xml:id="handout-section-2-4-partitioned-matrices">
  <title>2.4 Partitioned Matrices</title>

  <page xml:id="p-2-4-overview">
    <title>Overview</title>



    <objectives xml:id="obj-2-4-topics-goals">
      <title>Topics</title>
      <ul>
        <li>Partitioned matrices (or block matrices)</li>
      </ul>
    </objectives>

    <objectives>
      <title>Goals</title>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Apply partitioned matrices to problems involving matrix multiplication
          and invertibility.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </objectives>

    <p><nbsp/></p>

    <example xml:id="ex-2-4-basic-partition" workspace="true">
      <statement>
        <p>The matrix</p>
        <md>
          \begin{bmatrix}
            3 \amp 1 \amp 4 \amp 1 \amp 0\\
            1 \amp 6 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 1\\
            0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 4 \amp 2
          \end{bmatrix}
        </md>

        <p>can be partitioned into blocks:</p>

        <md>
          \begin{bmatrix}
            \begin{bmatrix}
              3 \amp 1 \amp 4\\
              1 \amp 6 \amp 1
            \end{bmatrix}
            \amp
            \begin{bmatrix}
              1 \amp 0\\
              0 \amp 1
            \end{bmatrix}
            \\
            \begin{bmatrix}
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0
            \end{bmatrix}
            \amp
            \begin{bmatrix}
              4 \amp 2
            \end{bmatrix}
          \end{bmatrix}
          =
          \begin{bmatrix}
            A_{11} \amp A_{12}\\
            A_{21} \amp A_{22}
          \end{bmatrix}.
        </md>

        <p>
          Each submatrix is called a <em>block</em>. Blocks can have different sizes.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-2-4-rref" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A row-reduced matrix can often be written in block form:
        </p>

        <md>
          \begin{bmatrix}
            1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp \ast \amp \cdots \amp \ast\\
            0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp \ast \amp \cdots \amp \ast\\
            0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp \ast \amp \cdots \amp \ast\\
            0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp \ast \amp \cdots \amp \ast\\
            0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp \cdots \amp 0\\
            0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp \cdots \amp 0
          \end{bmatrix}
          =
          \begin{bmatrix}
            I_4 \amp F\\
            0 \amp 0
          \end{bmatrix}.
        </md>

        <p>
          This form is helpful when studying the <em>null space</em> of a matrix.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>
  </page>

  <p><nbsp/></p>


  <page xml:id="p-2-4-row-column">
    <title>Row–Column Method</title>

    <p>
      Recall that multiplying a row vector by a column vector produces a scalar.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="ex-2-4-dot-product" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <md>
          \begin{bmatrix}1 \amp 1 \amp 1\end{bmatrix}
          \begin{bmatrix}1\\0\\2\end{bmatrix} = 
        </md>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
            Note that <md>
          \begin{bmatrix}1 \amp 1 \amp 1\end{bmatrix}
          \begin{bmatrix}1\\0\\2\end{bmatrix} = 1(1) + 1(0) + 1(2) = 1 + 0 + 2 = 3
        </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-row-column">
      <title>Row–Column Method</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>A</m> is <m>m\times n</m> and <m>B</m> is <m>n\times p</m>, then the
          <m>(i,j)</m> entry of <m>AB</m> is
        </p>
        <md>
          \text{row}_i(A)\cdot \text{col}_j(B).
        </md>
      </statement>
    </theorem>

    <p>
      Partitioned matrices can be multiplied the same way, treating each block like
      a single entry (provided dimensions match).
    </p>

    <example>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Let <m> A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 3 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}</m>                
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Verify that <m>A^2 = I_2</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    Note that <md>A^2 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 3 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 3 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 3 - 3 \amp (-1)^2 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix} = I_2</md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        <task workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use partitioned matrices to show that <m>M^2 = I_4</m>, where <md> M = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 3 \amp -1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp -3 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}</md>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    Note that <m>M</m> can be written as <md>M = \begin{bmatrix} A \amp 0_2 \\ I_2 \amp -A \end{bmatrix}</md>, where <m>0_2</m> is the <m>2 \times 2</m> zero matrix. Then, we have that <md>
                        <mrow> M^2 \amp = \begin{bmatrix} A \amp 0_2 \\ I_2 \amp -A\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix} A \amp 0_2 \\ I_2 \amp -A \end{bmatrix}</mrow> 
                        <mrow> \amp = \begin{bmatrix} A^2 + 0_2I_2 \amp A0_2 + 0_2(-A) \\ I_2A + -AI_2 \amp I_20_2 + (-A)(-A) \end{bmatrix}</mrow> 
                        <mrow> \amp = \begin{bmatrix} A^2 \amp 0_2 \\ A - A \amp A^2 \end{bmatrix}</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \begin{bmatrix} I_2 \amp 0_2 \\ 0_2 \amp I_2\end{bmatrix}</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = I_4</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </example>    
  </page>


  <page xml:id="p-2-4-block-inverse">
    <title>Example: Block Matrix Inverse</title>

    <example xml:id="ex-2-4-block-inverse" workspace="true">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>A,B,C</m> be invertible <m>n \times n </m> matrices.
          Find the inverse of:
        </p>
        <md>
          \begin{bmatrix}
            A \amp B\\
            0 \amp C
          \end{bmatrix}.
        </md>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
            We are looking for <m>n \times n</m> matrices <m>W,X,Y,Z</m> such that <md>\begin{bmatrix} A \amp B \\ 0 \amp C \end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix} W \amp X \\ Y \amp Z \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} I_2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp I_2 \end{bmatrix}</md>. Expanding the left side yields <md>\begin{bmatrix} AW + BY \amp AX + BZ \\ 0W + CY \amp 0X + CZ \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} I_2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp I_2 \end{bmatrix}</md>. Setting corresponding entries equal, we see that <md>
                <mrow> AW + BY \amp = I_2 </mrow>
                <mrow> AX + BZ \amp = 0</mrow>
                <mrow> CY \amp = 0 </mrow>
                <mrow> CZ \amp = I_2</mrow> 
            </md>
            Since <m>C</m> is invertible, multiplying the last two equations by <m>C^{-1}</m> on the left yields <m>Y = 0</m> and <m>Z = C^{-1}</m>. Plugging <m>Y = 0</m> into the top equation yields <md>AW + B0 = I_2 \quad \implies \quad AW = I_2 \quad \implies \quad W = A^{-1}</md>. Plugging <m>Z = C^{-1}</m> into the equation <m>AX + BZ = 0</m> yields <md>AX + BC^{-1} = 0 \ \implies \ AX = -BC^{-1} \ \implies \ X = -A^{-1}BC^{-1}</md>. Therefore, we have that <md>\begin{bmatrix} A \amp B \\ 0 \amp C \end{bmatrix}^{-1} = \begin{bmatrix} W \amp X \\ Y \amp Z \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} A^{-1} \amp -A^{-1}BC^{-1} \\ 0 \amp C^{-1}\end{bmatrix}</md>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Let's check. Note that <md>
                
                <mrow>\begin{bmatrix} A \amp B \\ 0 \amp C \end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}A \amp B \\ 0 \amp C \end{bmatrix}^{-1} \amp = \begin{bmatrix} A \amp B \\ 0 \amp C \end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}A^{-1} \amp -A^{-1}BC^{-1} \\ 0 \amp C^{-1} \end{bmatrix} </mrow> 
                <mrow> \amp = \begin{bmatrix} AA^{-1} + 0B \amp A(-A^{-1}BC^{-1}) + BC^{-1} \\ 0A^{-1} + C0 \amp 0(-A^{-1}BC^{-1}) + CC^{-1} \end{bmatrix}</mrow> 
                <mrow> \amp = \begin{bmatrix} I_2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp I_2 \end{bmatrix}</mrow>
            </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <p><nbsp/></p>

  </page>


  <page xml:id="p-2-4-strassen">
    <title>The Strassen Algorithm</title>

    <p>The following two examples demonstrate how partitioned matrices can be used. Students are not responsible for this material.</p>

    <example workspace="0.5in">
        <title>Strassen's Algorithm</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Standard multiplication of two <m>n\times n</m> matrices requires about <m>n^3</m> operations.
            </p>

            <p>
            Strassen’s algorithm partitions matrices and reduces the complexity to approximately <m>n^{2.803}</m>.
            </p>

            <image source="./StrassenAlgorithm.png" width="100%">
                <shortdescription>Visual representation of recursive block multiplication used in Strassen's algorithm.</shortdescription>
            </image>
        </statement>
    </example>
      
    <example workspace="0.5in"> 
        <title>Fast Fourier Transform</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                The Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) is another algorithm that uses partitioned matrices recursively.
            </p>

            <md>
                G_0 =
                \begin{bmatrix}1\end{bmatrix},
                \qquad
                G_{n+1} =
                \begin{bmatrix}
                    G_n \amp -G_n\\
                    G_n \amp G_n
                \end{bmatrix}.
            </md>

            <p>
            This recursive structure allows computation in nearly linear time,
            a dramatic improvement over <m>n^3</m>.
            </p>
        </statement> 
    </example>
  </page>

</handout>
